{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the hosted loop reads at head 4bc469ef, tree 5fa3fd9f, for request e63f921c and review request 2a830386. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED, for two documents only. The code, tests and evidence need no change.\n\n## Changes I request\n\n1. `docs/security/settings.md:700` still says: \"Uses narrow hosted defaults: create/install_application/credential/prepare/join/record/step/text omitted.\" On this head, that sentence is false. An operator who reads it believes a hosted model can't read full facts. Replace the list with the head's seven names, which also adds `diff`.\n\n2. The spent-secret sentence in `docs/mcp.md`, and the same decision in the note, describes only some Membership versions. See point 2 below.\n\n## Your four points\n\n1. Authority doesn't widen. `#step` and `#text` use the same `#reads.read(membership, …)` as the other reads, and `reads.ts` checks each read through `#open`. A limited reader still reads a retained input only if an entry it may read names that input. What widens is what the model sees. `steps` lines hold no fields, while `step` returns the whole signed intent, its presented facts, effects and sends. `text` also takes the kinds `definition`, `entry`, `rule`, `snapshot` and `value`. A member could already read all of this with a local client, so the planner's acceptance covers it, and the docs say so in general terms.\n\n2. The sentence is accurate only for older pins. `invitation-fields.ts` splits the pins:\n   - `platform:membership@1`, `@2`, `@4` and `@5` redeem with `secret`, so `step` shows it. The refusal holds: `invitationOpen` passes only `invited` and `lapsed`, and a redeemed item is neither. A `lapsed` one answers `invitation-expired`.\n   - `@6` to `@9` redeem with `proof`, a signature bound to the scope, the invitation and the joining key. Their issue act holds only `inviteKey`, a public key. So `step` shows no secret in a room on these pins, and \"shows ... the secret in a redeemed `join` or `enrol`\" is false there.\n\n   The risk is smaller than the document says, so the planner's acceptance in 2745582c still holds. Say which pins show the secret and that the others show a proof.\n\n   I also tried the digest route for an unredeemed secret. On older pins, `inviteHash` is `textDigest(secret)`, and `text` reads by digest, so a retained secret would be readable through it. The CLI's issue path sends only the hash and no detached text, so no such text is retained. I read this path and did not run it. An act that lands in the log is accepted, because an `act` input has no refused form, so a refused `join` adds no entry.\n\n3. Yes, that is enough. C1 and C2 stop at the list assertion, and the case above it shows that a tool off the list refuses a direct call. I read every value that reaches the agent's tools. Each comes from its own `tool` calls or its own act answers. Rita supplies state only, and the case uses her reads to find the approval request for her own act. One limit remains: the case's entries fit one page, so `step`'s paged `json` form isn't in this path. `factOf` would fail on it, but an agent reads `hash` on any page. This doesn't block.\n\n4. Accept the sentence as guidance, and don't count it as a control. The controls are the member's grants and the people who hold approval and merge. A model that obeys hostile text can still sign anything its own role allows, in hosted and local sessions alike. The sentence reads correctly in both places, and `wire.test.ts` asserts both.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The gate at 47958832, tree 12c18962, exits 0: Plain 3,217 passed with one skip, Native 898 with two skips. From there to this head, the only changes are the note, the manifest and the merge of main.\n- `git merge-tree --write-tree` of 98fc1192 and this head gives this head's tree.\n- The evidence verifies. All 10 manifest rows match, the directory holds 44 files with none writable, and `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest 2fafa988.\n- All five control logs say that they distinguish. I read them and did not rerun them.\n- I ran each once: `hosted-tools.test.ts` and `wire.test.ts`, 18 of 18 across both; the two hosted default cases in `hosted.scope.test.ts`, 2 of 2; and `security-entry-points.test.mjs`, 10 of 10. `check-commit-messages` checks 7 with 0 flagged, `git diff --check` is clean, and the worktree stayed clean.\n- No deployment sets `MCP_TOOL_ADDITIONS` to a list that this change breaks. Celld sets `[]`, which now gives the new default.\n\nI ran no live hosted agent and no second independent read.\n\n## Architecture\n\nA one-list change with the existing read path. Approved in substance. The verdict waits on the two documents.\n\n## Security\n\nNo authority widens, and the model sees more within the same member read. The documentation errors are what block: one security page states the old default, and one sentence names a secret that current pins don't log.\n\n## Simplification\n\n`record` stays out, and the head adds no new read code. Approved.\n\n## Next head\n\nA documents-only change needs no new gate. Keep the seals as they are, since neither file is sealed source.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:255731a3b99bc09d88d3873697aff67d679c90f5","binding":"assigned","head":"4bc469ef10cb6ead8cfa44d7596c7039435a54d1","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:499b970bfbcdac36f62a35e9aaab8419699f7ae0\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2d3ccd1fe2bcd962578424884e4771eca8f0b9a8\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:499b970bfbcdac36f62a35e9aaab8419699f7ae0","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}